\subsection{Theorems}
\label{sec-theorems}



\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Theorem 1.}\label{thm-fermat} \itshape{} \subsubsection{Fermat's Last Theorem}
\label{fermats-last-theorem}

For $n > 2$, there are no positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ such that $a^n + b^n = c^n$.

\upshape\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textit{ Proof.} The proof was completed by Andrew Wiles in 1995 and is too long to include here.

 \hfill$\square$\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Definition 1.}\label{def-group} \subsubsection{Group}
\label{group}

A group is a set $G$ with a binary operation satisfying closure, associativity, identity, and invertibility.

\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Lemma 1.}\label{lem-helper} \itshape{} Every finite group has an element of order dividing the group order.

\upshape\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Corollary 1.}\label{cor-consequence} \itshape{} Every group of prime order is cyclic.

\upshape\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Example 1.}\label{exm-integers} The integers under addition form a group.

\medskip

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Remark 1.}\label{rem-note} Groups are fundamental to abstract algebra.

\medskip

By \hyperref[thm-fermat]{Theorem 1} and \hyperref[def-group]{Definition 1}, we see the connection. Also see \hyperref[lem-helper]{Lemma 1}, \hyperref[cor-consequence]{Corollary 1}, \hyperref[exm-integers]{Example 1}, and \hyperref[rem-note]{Remark 1}.

